at are called  effective behavioral interventions and  those are interventions that CDC that  they've been studied they've been  replicated and they've been shown to  actual  change people's behaviors you know  because for many years we did a lot of  programs that you know they had good  intentions but because at you know at  the time HIV was relatively new we  didn't have a lot of research to back up  what we were doing other than are we  reaching people are we talking to them  do they seem to be learning stuff but we  really didn't know if it was leading to  actual behavior change which was up is  you know ultimately necessary to reduce  new HIV infections so you know but then  on the other hand when we looked at  these affected behavioral interventions  there really wasn't a lot specifically  targeting MSM so there was kind of like  this you know kind of struggle with you  know on the one hand saying we are going  to fund effective behavioral  interventions because they've been  proven to be effective and on the other  hand you know communities going you know  what we see them we understand them but  they're not really going to work in our  community because you know we're not set  up like Chicago or New York or wherever  where these things may have been studied  and so you know I was driving back and I  thought oh wait a minute you know we  have some state funding let's take some  of that state funding and pull it out of  the big pool and we will target it to  those two groups MSM and ID you and  because it's state funding it's not tied  to our federal grant we don't have to be  so worried about adhering to you know  these affected behavioral interventions  that CDC sort of you know really pushes  us to use and if we don't use them we  have to go through this crazy stuff so  it's you know I'm not going to go there  describing that but you know the state  funding actually gave us the freedom to  allow communities to kind of be creative  we we asked them to do an 